                                                </mtext></mrow><mo class="ltx_mathvariant_italic" lspace="0em" mathvariant="italic" separator="true">∎</mo></mrow></math></span></p> 
</div> 
</div> 
</section> 
<section class="ltx_subsection">  
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection" style="font-size:70%;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">C.2 </span>Proof of Corollary <span class="ltx_ref ltx_missing_label ltx_ref_self"></span>: Dominating negatively interfering prompts</h3>
 
<div class="ltx_proof"> 
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_font_italic ltx_title_proof" style="font-size:70%;">Proof.</h6> 
<div class="ltx_para"> 
<p class="ltx_p"><span class="ltx_text" style="font-size:70%;">First, Proposition <span class="ltx_ref ltx_missing_label ltx_ref_self"></span> yields:</span></p> 
<table class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="0" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(24)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table> 
<p class="ltx_p"><span class="ltx_text" style="font-size:70%;">We decompose this inner product into 2 regions: the <math alttext="m" display="inline"><mi>m</mi></math>-strongly negatively interfering region <math alttext="\mathcal{X}_{-}(\theta,m)" display="inline"><mrow><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒳</mi><mo>−</mo></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>θ</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>m</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></math> and its complement,</span></p> 
<table class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="0" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(25)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table> 
</div> 
<div class="ltx_para"> 
<p class="ltx_p"><span class="ltx_text" style="font-size:70%;">We now upperbound each one of the above terms. For the first term, by definition of the set of prompts <math alttext="\mathcal{X}_{-}(\theta,m)" display="inline"><mrow><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒳</mi><mo>−</mo></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>θ</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>m</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></math>, we have <math alttext="a_{\theta}(x)\leq-m" display="inline"><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>a</mi><mi>θ</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>≤</mo><mrow><mo>−</mo><mi>m</mi></mrow></mrow></math> for any <math alttext="x\in\mathcal{X}_{-}(\theta,m)\,." display="inline"><mrow><mrow><mi>x</mi><mo>∈</mo><mrow><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒳</mi><mo>−</mo></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>θ</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>m</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0.170em">.</mo></mrow></math> As for the second term, we show that for any <math alttext="x\notin\mathcal{X}_{-}(\theta,m)," display="inline"><mrow><mrow><mi>x</mi><mo>∉</mo><mrow><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒳</mi><mo>−</mo></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>θ</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>m</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow></math> <math alttext="|a_{\theta}(x)|\leq G^{2}" display="inline"><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo><mrow><msub><mi>a</mi><mi>θ</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow><mo>≤</mo><msup><mi>G</mi><mn>2</mn></msup></mrow></math> using Assumption <span class="ltx_ref ltx_missing_label ltx_ref_self"></span> as follows.</span></p> 
</div> 
<div class="ltx_para ltx_noindent"> 
<p class="ltx_p"><span class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold" style="font-size:70%;">Bounding <math alttext="a_{\theta}(x)." display="inline"><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>a</mi><mi>θ</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow></math></span><span class="ltx_text" style="font-size:70%;"> Using the definition of the agreement score </span><math alttext="a_{\theta}(x)" display="inline"><mrow><msub><mi mathsize="0.700em">a</mi><mi mathsize="0.700em">θ</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo maxsize="0.700em" minsize="0.700em">(</mo><mi mathsize="0.700em">x</mi><mo maxsize="0.700em" minsize="0.700em">)</mo></mrow></mrow></math><span class="ltx_text" style="font-size:70%;"> (see (</span><span class="ltx_ref ltx_missing_label ltx_ref_self" style="font-size:70%;"></span><span class="ltx_text" style="font-size:70%;">)) together with Cauchy-Schwarz inequality, we have</span></p> 
<table class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="0" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(26)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table> 
<p class="ltx_p"><span class="ltx_text" style="font-size:70%;">Then recalling the policy gradient expression of </span><math alttext="\nabla J_{1}(x;\theta)" display="inline"><mrow><mrow><mo mathsize="0.700em" rspace="0.167em">∇</mo><msub><mi mathsize="0.700em">J</mi><mn mathsize="0.700em">1</mn></msub></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo maxsize="0.700em" minsize="0.700em">(</mo><mi mathsize="0.700em">x</mi><mo mathsize="0.700em">,</mo><mi mathsize="0.700em">θ</mi><mo maxsize="0.700em" minsize="0.700em">)</mo></mrow></mrow></math><span class="ltx_text" style="font-size:70%;"> in (</span><a href="#S2.E4" title="Equation 4 ‣ 2 Preliminaries ‣ Why Pass@k Optimization Can Degrade Pass@1: Prompt Interference in LLM Post-training" class="ltx_ref" style="font-size:70%;"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">4</span></a><span class="ltx_text" style="font-size:70%;">) and using Jensen’s inequality, we have:</span></p> 
<table class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="0" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(27)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table> 
<p class="ltx_p"><span class="ltx_text" style="font-size:70%;">where the last inequality follows from using Assumption </span><span class="ltx_ref ltx_missing_label ltx_ref_self" style="font-size:70%;"></span><span class="ltx_text" style="font-size:70%;">. Using this inequality in (</span><span class="ltx_ref ltx_missing_label ltx_ref_self" style="font-size:70%;"></span><span class="ltx_text" style="font-size:70%;">) yields the desired inequality:</span></p> 
<table class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="0" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(28)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table> 
<p class="ltx_p"><span class="ltx_text" style="font-size:70%;">Back to (</span><span class="ltx_ref ltx_missing_label ltx_ref_self" style="font-size:70%;"></span><span class="ltx_text" style="font-size:70%;">) and using the inequalities established above, we conclude the proof:</span></p> 
<table class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="0" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(29)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table> 
<p class="ltx_p"><span class="ltx_text" style="font-size:70%;">where </span><math alttext="\delta(\theta):=mW_{-}(k,\theta)-G^{2}W_{+}(k,\theta)" display="inline"><mrow><mrow><mi mathsize="0.700em">δ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo maxsize="0.700em" minsize="0.700em">(</mo><mi mathsize="0.700em">θ</mi><mo maxsize="0.700em" minsize="0.700em">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo mathsize="0.700em">:=</mo><mrow><mrow><mi mathsize="0.700em">m</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi mathsize="0.700em">W</mi><mo mathsize="0.700em">−</mo></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo maxsize="0.700em" minsize="0.700em">(</mo><mi mathsize="0.700em">k</mi><mo mathsize="0.700em">,</mo><mi mathsize="0.700em">θ</mi><mo maxsize="0.700em" minsize="0.700em">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo mathsize="0.700em">−</mo><mrow><msup><mi mathsize="0.700em">G</mi><mn mathsize="0.700em">2</mn></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi mathsize="0.700em">W</mi><mo mathsize="0.700em">+</mo></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo maxsize="0.700em" minsize="0.700em">(</mo><mi mathsize="0.700em">k</mi><mo mathsize="0.700em">,</mo><mi mathsize="0.700em">θ</mi><mo maxsize="0.700em" minsize="0.700em">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow></math><span class="ltx_text" style="font-size:70%;"> and the aggregated weights </span><math alttext="W_{-}(k,\theta),W_{+}(k,\theta)" display="inline"><mrow><mrow><msub><mi mathsize="0.700em">W</mi><mo mathsize="0.700em">−</mo></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo maxsize="0.700em" minsize="0.700em">(</mo><mi mathsize="0.700em">k</mi><mo mathsize="0.700em">,</mo><mi mathsize="0.700em">θ</mi><mo maxsize="0.700em" minsize="0.700em">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo mathsize="0.700em">,</mo><mrow><msub><mi mathsize="0.700em">W</mi><mo mathsize="0.700em">+</mo></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo maxsize="0.700em" minsize="0.700em">(</mo><mi mathsize="0.700em">k</mi><mo mathsize="0.700em">,</mo><mi mathsize="0.700em">θ</mi><mo maxsize="0.700em" minsize="0.700em">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></math><span class="ltx_text" style="font-size:70%;"> are defined in (</span><span class="ltx_ref ltx_missing_label ltx_ref_self" style="font-size:70%;"></span><span class="ltx_text" style="font-size:70%;">). If in addition </span><math alttext="\delta(\theta)&lt;0" display="inline"><mrow><mrow><mi mathsize="0.700em">δ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo maxsize="0.700em" minsize="0.700em">(</mo><mi mathsize="0.700em">θ</mi><mo maxsize="0.700em" minsize="0.700em">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo mathsize="0.700em">&lt;</mo><mn mathsize="0.700em">0</mn></mrow></math><span class="ltx_text" style="font-size:70%;">, then </span><math alttext="\langle\nabla J_{k}(\theta),\nabla J_{1}(\theta)\rangle\leq-\delta(\theta)&lt;0\,." display="inline"><mrow><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">⟨</mo><mrow><mrow><mrow><mo mathsize="0.700em" rspace="0.167em">∇</mo><msub><mi mathsize="0.700em">J</mi><mi mathsize="0.700em">k</mi></msub></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo maxsize="0.700em" minsize="0.700em">(</mo><mi mathsize="0.700em">θ</mi><mo maxsize="0.700em" minsize="0.700em">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo mathsize="0.700em">,</mo><mrow><mrow><mo mathsize="0.700em" rspace="0.167em">∇</mo><msub><mi mathsize="0.700em">J</mi><mn mathsize="0.700em">1</mn></msub></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo maxsize="0.700em" minsize="0.700em">(</mo><mi mathsize="0.700em">θ</mi><mo maxsize="0.700em" minsize="0.700em">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">⟩</mo></mrow><mo mathsize="0.700em">≤</mo><mrow><mo mathsize="0.700em">−</mo><mrow><mi mathsize="0.700em">δ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo maxsize="0.700em" minsize="0.700em">(</mo><mi mathsize="0.700em">θ</mi><mo maxsize="0.700em" minsize="0.700em">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo mathsize="0.700em">&lt;</mo><mn mathsize="0.700em">0</mn></mrow><mo lspace="0.170em" mathsize="0.700em">.</mo></mrow></math><span class="ltx_text" style="font-size:70%;"> ∎</span></p> 
</div> 
</div> 
</section> 
<section class="ltx_subsection">  
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection" style="font-size:70%;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">C.3 </span>Proof of Proposition <span class="ltx_ref ltx_missing_label ltx_ref_self"></span>: Influence of <math alttext="k" display="inline"><mi>k</mi></math></h3>
 
<div class="ltx_proof"> 
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_font_italic ltx_title_proof" style="font-size:70%;">Proof.</h6> 
<div class="ltx_para"> 
<p class="ltx_p"><span class="ltx_text" style="font-size:70%;">Recall from Proposition <span class="ltx_ref ltx_missing_label ltx_ref_self"></span> that:</span></p> 
<table class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="0" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(30)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table> 
<p class="ltx_p"><span class="ltx_text" style="font-size:70%;">Using again the same decomposition as in (<span class="ltx_ref ltx_missing_label ltx_ref_self"></span>), we have:</span></p> 
<table class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="0" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(31)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table> 
<p class="ltx_p"><span class="ltx_text" style="font-size:70%;">Depending on the prompt region, we have two distinct ways to bound the pass@<math alttext="k" display="inline"><mi>k</mi></math> weights:</span></p> 
<ol class="ltx_enumerate" style="--ltx-enum-leftmargin:0pt;"> 
<li class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">(i)</span> 
<div class="ltx_para"> 
<p class="ltx_p"><span class="ltx_text" style="font-size:70%;">For </span><math alttext="x\in\mathcal{X}(m,\theta)" display="inline"><mrow><mi mathsize="0.700em">x</mi><mo mathsize="0.700em">∈</mo><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic" mathsize="0.700em">𝒳</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo maxsize="0.700em" minsize="0.700em">(</mo><mi mathsize="0.700em">m</mi><mo mathsize="0.700em">,</mo><mi mathsize="0.700em">θ</mi><mo maxsize="0.700em" minsize="0.700em">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></math><span class="ltx_text" style="font-size:70%;">, we have by assumption, </span><math alttext="p_{\theta}(x)\leq\varepsilon" display="inline"><mrow><mrow><msub><mi mathsize="0.700em">p</mi><mi mathsize="0.700em">θ</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo maxsize="0.700em" minsize="0.700em">(</mo><mi mathsize="0.700em">x</mi><mo maxsize="0.700em" minsize="0.700em">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo mathsize="0.700em">≤</mo><mi mathsize="0.700em">ε</mi></mrow></math><span class="ltx_text" style="font-size:70%;">, hence </span><math alttext="\omega_{k,\theta}(x)\ \geq\ k(1-\varepsilon)^{k-1}." display="inline"><mrow><mrow><mrow><msub><mi mathsize="0.700em">ω</mi><mrow><mi mathsize="0.700em">k</mi><mo mathsize="0.700em">,</mo><mi mathsize="0.700em">θ</mi></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo maxsize="0.700em" minsize="0.700em">(</mo><mi mathsize="0.700em">x</mi><mo maxsize="0.700em" minsize="0.700em" rspace="0.410em">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo mathsize="0.700em" rspace="0.688em">≥</mo><mrow><mi mathsize="0.700em">k</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mrow><mo maxsize="0.700em" minsize="0.700em">(</mo><mrow><mn mathsize="0.700em">1</mn><mo mathsize="0.700em">−</mo><mi mathsize="0.700em">ε</mi></mrow><mo maxsize="0.700em" minsize="0.700em">)</mo></mrow><mrow><mi mathsize="0.700em">k</mi><mo mathsize="0.700em">−</mo><mn mathsize="0.700em">1</mn></mrow></msup></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em" mathsize="0.700em">.</mo></mrow></math></p> 
</div></li> 
<li class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">(ii)</span> 
<div class="ltx_para"> 
<p class="ltx_p"><span class="ltx_text" style="font-size:70%;">For </span><math alttext="x\notin\mathcal{X}(m,\theta)" display="inline"><mrow><mi mathsize="0.700em">x</mi><mo mathsize="0.700em">∉</mo><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic" mathsize="0.700em">𝒳</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo maxsize="0.700em" minsize="0.700em">(</mo><mi mathsize="0.700em">m</mi><mo mathsize="0.700em">,</mo><mi mathsize="0.700em">θ</mi><mo maxsize="0.700em" minsize="0.700em">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></math><span class="ltx_text" style="font-size:70%;"> we have by assumption, </span><math alttext="p_{\theta}(x)\geq\delta" display="inline"><mrow><mrow><msub><mi mathsize="0.700em">p</mi><mi mathsize="0.700em">θ</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo maxsize="0.700em" minsize="0.700em">(</mo><mi mathsize="0.700em">x</mi><mo maxsize="0.700em" minsize="0.700em">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo mathsize="0.700em">≥</mo><mi mathsize="0.700em">δ</mi></mrow></math><span class="ltx_text" style="font-size:70%;">, hence </span><math alttext="\omega_{k,\theta}(x)\ \leq\ k(1-\delta)^{k-1}." display="inline"><mrow><mrow><mrow><msub><mi mathsize="0.700em">ω</mi><mrow><mi mathsize="0.700em">k</mi><mo mathsize="0.700em">,</mo><mi mathsize="0.700em">θ</mi></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo maxsize="0.700em" minsize="0.700em">(</mo><mi mathsize="0.700em">x</mi><mo maxsize="0.700em" minsize="0.700em" rspace="0.410em">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo mathsize="0.700em" rspace="0.688em">≤</mo><mrow><mi mathsize="0.700em">k</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mrow><mo maxsize="0.700em" minsize="0.700em">(</mo><mrow><mn mathsize="0.700em">1</mn><mo mathsize="0.700em">−</mo><mi mathsize="0.700em">δ</mi></mrow><mo maxsize="0.700em" minsize="0.700em">)</mo></mrow><mrow><mi mathsize="0.700em">k</mi><mo mathsize="0.700em">−</mo><mn mathsize="0.700em">1</mn></mrow></msup></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em" mathsize="0.700em">.</mo></mrow></math></p> 
</div></li> 
</ol> 
<p class="ltx_p"><span class="ltx_text" style="font-size:70%;">Observing in addition to both items above that <math alttext="a_{\theta}(x)\leq-m" display="inline"><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>a</mi><mi>θ</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>≤</mo><mrow><mo>−</mo><mi>m</mi></mrow></mrow></math> for any <math alttext="x\in\mathcal{X}_{-}(\theta,m)" display="inline"><mrow><mi>x</mi><mo>∈</mo><mrow><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒳</mi><mo>−</mo></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>θ</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>m</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></math> and <math alttext="a_{\theta}(x)\leq G^{2}" display="inline"><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>a</mi><mi>θ</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>≤</mo><msup><mi>G</mi><mn>2</mn></msup></mrow></math> for any <math alttext="x\in\mathcal{X}" display="inline"><mrow><mi>x</mi><mo>∈</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒳</mi></mrow></math> (as shown in (<span class="ltx_ref ltx_missing_label ltx_ref_self"></span>) under Assumption <span class="ltx_ref ltx_missing_label ltx_ref_self"></span>) and hence for any <math alttext="x\notin\mathcal{X}_{-}(\theta,m)" display="inline"><mrow><mi>x</mi><mo>∉</mo><mrow><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒳</mi><mo>−</mo></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>θ</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>m</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></math> in particular, we obtain from (<span class="ltx_ref ltx_missing_label ltx_ref_self"></span>) the following inequality:</span></p> 
<table class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="0" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(32)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table> 
<p class="ltx_p"><span class="ltx_text" style="font-size:70%;">where we also use the assumption <math alttext="q_{\theta}&gt;q\,." display="inline"><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>q</mi><mi>θ</mi></msub><mo>&gt;</mo><mi>q</mi></mrow><mo lspace="0.170em">.</mo></mrow></math> Thus it suffices to have</span></p> 
<table class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table> 
<p class="ltx_p"><span class="ltx_text" style="font-size:70%;">to ensure that <math alttext="\bigl\langle\nabla J_{k}(\theta),\nabla J_{1}(\theta)\bigr\rangle&lt;0\,." display="inline"><mrow><mrow><mrow><mo maxsize="1.430em" minsize="1.430em" stretchy="true">⟨</mo><mrow><mrow><mrow><mo rspace="0.167em">∇</mo><msub><mi>J</mi><mi>k</mi></msub></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>θ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo><mrow><mrow><mo rspace="0.167em">∇</mo><msub><mi>J</mi><mn>1</mn></msub></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>θ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo maxsize="1.430em" minsize="1.430em" stretchy="true">⟩</mo></mrow><mo>&lt;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><mo lspace="0.170em">.</mo></mrow></math> The above condition is equivalent to:</span></p> 
<table class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table> 
<p class="ltx_p"><span class="ltx_text" style="font-size:70%;">Taking <math alttext="\log" display="inline"><mi>log</mi></math> in the above inequality, recalling that <math alttext="1-\varepsilon&gt;1-\delta" display="inline"><mrow><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>−</mo><mi>ε</mi></mrow><mo>&gt;</mo><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>−</mo><mi>δ</mi></mrow></mrow></math> and reordering the inequality yields:</span></p> 
<table class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="0" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(33)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table> 
<p class="ltx_p"><span class="ltx_text" style="font-size:70%;">which concludes the proof.
∎</span></p> 
</div> 
</div> 
</section> 
<section class="ltx_subsection">  
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection" style="font-size:70%;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">C.4 </span>Lemma <span class="ltx_ref ltx_missing_label ltx_ref_self"></span>: Smoothness of pass@<math alttext="k" display="inline"><mi>k</mi></math></h3>
 
<div class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_lemma">  
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem" style="font-size:70%;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Lemma C.1</span></span><span class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold"> </span>(Smoothness of pass@<math alttext="k" display="inline"><mi>k</mi></math>)<span class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6> 
<div class="ltx_para"> 
<p class="ltx_p"><span class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic" style="font-size:70%;">Under Assumption <span class="ltx_ref ltx_missing_label ltx_ref_self"></span>, for all k </span><span class="ltx_text" style="font-size:70%;">≥</span><span class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic" style="font-size:70%;">1<math alttext=",thepass@" class="ltx_math_unparsed" display="inline"><mrow><mo>,</mo><mi>t</mi><mi>h</mi><mi>e</mi><mi>p</mi><mi>a</mi><mi>s</mi><mi>s</mi><mi mathvariant="normal">@</mi></mrow></math>k<math alttext="objective" display="inline"><mrow><mi>o</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>b</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>j</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>e</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>c</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>t</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>i</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>v</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>e</mi></mrow></math>J_k<math alttext="is" display="inline"><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>s</mi></mrow></math>L_k<math alttext="-smoothwith" display="inline"><mrow><mo>−</mo><mrow><mi>s</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>m</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>o</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>o</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>t</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>h</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>w</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>i</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>t</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>h</mi></mrow></mrow></math>L_k := k^2 G^2 + kF,<math alttext="i.e.forall" display="inline"><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0.167em">.</mo><mi>e</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0.167em">.</mo><mrow><mi>f</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>o</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>r</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>a</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>l</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>l</mi></mrow></mrow></math>θ, θ’ </span><span class="ltx_text" style="font-size:70%;">∈</span><span class="ltx_text ltx_font_blackboard" style="font-size:70%;">R</span><span class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic" style="font-size:70%;">^d,<math alttext="\begin{equation*}|J_{k}(\theta^{\prime})-J_{k}(\theta)-\langle\nabla J_{k}(\theta),\theta^{\prime}-\theta\rangle|\leq\frac{L_{k}}{2}\|\theta^{\prime}-\theta\|_{2}^{2}\,.\end{equation*}\end{lemma}\par\@proof Toshowsmoothnessofthepass@$k$ objective $J_{k}$, we bound its Hessian $\nabla^{2}J_{k}(\theta)$ uniformly in the policy parameter\penalty\ $\theta$ using Assumption\penalty\ \ref{as:E-LS}.
\par Since
$\nabla J_{k}(\theta)=\mathbb{E}_{x\sim\mathcal{D}}[w_{k}(p_{\theta}(x))\nabla p_{\theta}(x)]$ as shown in \eqref{eq:pass@k-grad}, the chain rule gives the Hessian of $J_{k}$ for every $\theta\in\mathbb{R}^{d}$,
\begin{equation}\nabla^{2}J_{k}(\theta)=\mathbb{E}_{x\sim\mathcal{D}}\Big[\omega_{k}^{\prime}(p_{\theta}(x))\,\nabla p_{\theta}(x)\,\nabla p_{\theta}(x)^{\top}+\omega_{k}(p_{\theta}(x))\,\nabla^{2}p_{\theta}(x)\Big].\end{equation}
Taking the operator norm and using the triangular inequality yields:
\begin{equation}\big\|\nabla^{2}J_{k}(\theta)\big\|\leq\mathbb{E}_{x\sim\mathcal{D}}\Big[\big|\omega_{k}^{\prime}(p_{\theta}(x))\big|\cdot\,\|\nabla p_{\theta}(x)\|^{2}+\big|\omega_{k}(p_{\theta}(x))\big|\cdot\,\|\nabla^{2}p_{\theta}(x)\|\Big].\end{equation}
In the rest of the proof, we bound each term separately.
\par\par\@@unnumbered@section{paragraph}{toc}{Step 1: Bounding $\|\nabla p_{\theta}(x)\|^2$.}
Recall from \eqref{eq:pass@1pg} that the pass@1 policy gradient is given by:
\begin{equation}\nabla p_{\theta}(x)=\mathbb{E}_{y\sim\pi_{\theta}(\cdot|x)}\!\Big[r(x,y)\,\nabla\log\pi_{\theta}(y|x)\Big].\end{equation}
By Jensen's inequality and since $r(x,y)\in\{0,1\}$, we have
\@@amsalign
 where the last bound follows from Assumption\penalty\ \ref{as:E-LS}.
\par\par\@@unnumbered@section{paragraph}{toc}{Step 2: Bounding $\|\nabla^2 p_{\theta}(x)\|$.}
Differentiating the pass@1 policy gradient, we obtain:
$$\nabla^{2}p_{\theta}(x)=\mathbb{E}_{y\sim\pi_{\theta}(\cdot|x)}\!\Big[r(x,y)\,\nabla\log\pi_{\theta}(y|x)\,\nabla\log\pi_{\theta}(y|x)^{\top}+r(x,y)\,\nabla^{2}\log\pi_{\theta}(y|x)\Big].$$
Taking the operator norm and using the triangle inequality, we obtain:
\@@amsalign
 where the last inequality again follows from using Assumption\penalty\ \ref{as:E-LS}.
\par\par\@@unnumbered@section{paragraph}{toc}{Step 3: Bounding weights $\omega_k'$ and $\omega_k$.} By definition of the weights $w_{k}(p)=f_{k}^{\prime}(p)=k(1-p)^{k-1}$ for any $p\in[0,1]$. Therefore it follows that $\omega_{k}^{\prime}(p)=-k(k-1)(1-p)^{k-2}$ for $k\geq 2$. Hence, for any $p\in[0,1]$ and any $k\geq 2$,
\begin{equation}|\omega_{k}(p)|\leq k\,,\hskip 8.19447pt|\omega_{k}^{\prime}(p)|\leq k(k-1)\,.\end{equation}
\par\par\@@unnumbered@section{paragraph}{toc}{Conclusion.}
Plugging \eqref{eq:grad-e-bound}--\eqref{eq:hess-e-bound} and the bounds on $\omega_{k},\omega_{k}^{\prime}$ \eqref{eq:weights-bounds} into \eqref{eq:hess-norm-split} yields
$$\|\nabla^{2}J_{k}(\theta)\|\leq k(k-1)\,G^{2}+k\,(G^{2}+F)\leq k^{2}G^{2}+kF.$$
Finally, boundedness of the Hessian norm implies that $J_{k}$ is
$L_{k}$-smooth with $L_{k}=k^{2}G^{2}+kF$, i.e., for any $\theta,\theta^{\prime}\in\mathbb{R}^{d}\,,$
$$\Big|J_{k}(\theta^{\prime})-J_{k}(\theta)-\langle\nabla J_{k}(\theta),\theta^{\prime}-\theta\rangle\Big|\leq\frac{L_{k}}{2}\|\theta^{\prime}-\theta\|^{2}.$$
\qed\end@proof
\par\par\@@numbered@section{subsection}{toc}{Proof of Proposition~\ref{prop:pass@1-degradation}: Pass@$1$ degradation under pass@$k$ updates}
\par\@proof We prove the decrease of pass@1 and increase of pass@$k$underpass@$k$policygradientascentseparately.Recallthepolicyupdaterulewithstepsize$\eta&gt; 0$:\begin{equation}\theta^{+}=\theta+\eta\,\nabla J_{k}(\theta).\end{equation}\par\noindent\textbf{(i) Pass@1 descent.}By$L_1$-smoothnessof$J_1$(Lemma\penalty\ \ref{lem:smoothness-pass@k})andusingthepolicyupdaterule\penalty\ \eqref{eq:pass@k-gd},wehave:\begin{aligned} J_{1}(\theta^{+})&amp;\leq J_{1}(\theta)+\langle\nabla J_{1}(\theta),\theta^{+}-\theta\rangle+\frac{L_{1}}{2}\|\theta^{+}-\theta\|^{2}\\
&amp;=J_{1}(\theta)+\eta\,\langle\nabla J_{1}(\theta),\nabla J_{k}(\theta)\rangle+\frac{L_{1}\eta^{2}}{2}\|\nabla J_{k}(\theta)\|^{2}.\end{aligned}\par Wenowboundeachoneofthelasttwotermsintheaboveinequality.\par\noindent\textbf{Bounding $\|\nabla J_{k}(\theta)\|^{2}$.}Recallthepass@$k$policygradientexpression$\nabla J_k(\theta)
= \mathbb{E}_{x}\!\left[\omega_\theta(x)\,\nabla J_1(x;\theta)\right]$,wehavebyJensenandCauchy-Schwarzinequalities:\begin{equation}\|\nabla J_{k}(\theta)\|^{2}=\left\|\mathbb{E}_{x}\!\left[\omega_{\theta}(x)\,\nabla J_{1}(x;\theta)\right]\right\|^{2}\;\leq\;\mathbb{E}_{x}\!\left[|\omega_{\theta}(x)|^{2}\,\|\nabla J_{1}(x;\theta)\|^{2}\right]\leq k^{2}G^{2}\,,\end{equation}wherethelastinequalityfollowsfromusingthepass@1policygradientboundin\eqref{eq:pass@1-grad-bound}togetherwithimmediateweightbound$|\omega_\theta(x)|\leq k$andthefactthattherewardisbinary(thiscanbegeneralizedtoacontinuousuniformlyboundedrewardfunction).\par\noindent\textbf{Bounding $\langle\nabla J_{1}(\theta),\nabla J_{k}(\theta)\rangle$.}ByCorollary\penalty\ \ref{cor:dominating-neg-interfer-prompts},wehave\begin{equation}\langle\nabla J_{1}(\theta),\nabla J_{k}(\theta)\rangle\leq-\delta(\theta)\,.\end{equation}Combiningthebounds\penalty\ \eqref{eq:grad-bound-1}and\eqref{eq:innerprod-bound},weimmediatelyobtainfrom\eqref{eq:smooth-J1}:\begin{equation}J_{1}(\theta^{+})\leq J_{1}(\theta)-\eta\delta(\theta)+C_{2}\eta^{2}\,,\end{equation}where$C_2 := \frac{L_{1} k^{2} G^{2}}{2}$and$L_1 = G^2 + F$asshowninLemma\penalty\ \ref{lem:smoothness-pass@k},$G, F$beingsmoothnessconstantsdefinedinAssumption\penalty\ \ref{as:E-LS}.\par Hence,if$\delta(\theta) &gt; 0$and$0 &lt; \eta\leq\eta_+ := \frac{\delta(\theta)}{C_{2}},$then$-\eta\delta(\theta) + C_2\eta^2&lt;0$anditfollowsthat\begin{equation}J_{1}(\theta^{+})\leq J_{1}(\theta)-\eta\delta(\theta)+C_{2}\eta^{2}&lt;J_{1}(\theta)\,,\end{equation}whichisthefirstdesiredinequality.\par\noindent\textbf{(ii) Pass@$k$ ascent.}Asforpass@$k$underpass@$k$policygradientascent,theproofisastandardapplicationofsmoothnessinoptimization.Weprovideabriefproofinoursettingforcompleteness.\par Using$L_k$-smoothnessofthepass@$k$objectiveshowninLemma\penalty\ \ref{lem:smoothness-pass@k}togetherwiththepolicyupdaterule\penalty\ \eqref{eq:pass@k-gd},wehave:\begin{aligned} J_{k}(\theta^{+})&amp;\geq J_{k}(\theta)+\langle\nabla J_{k}(\theta),\theta^{+}-\theta\rangle-\frac{L_{k}}{2}\|\theta^{+}-\theta\|^{2}\\
&amp;=J_{k}(\theta)+\eta\|\nabla J_{k}(\theta)\|^{2}-\frac{L_{k}\eta^{2}}{2}\|\nabla J_{k}(\theta)\|^{2}\\
&amp;=J_{k}(\theta)+\eta\Bigl(1-\frac{L_{k}\eta}{2}\Bigr)\|\nabla J_{k}(\theta)\|^{2}.\end{aligned}Wepickthen$\eta\leq\frac{1}{L_{k}}$toobtaintheseconddesiredinequality:\begin{equation}J_{k}(\theta^{+})\geq J_{k}(\theta)+\frac{\eta}{2}\|\nabla J_{k}(\theta)\|^{2}\geq J_{k}(\theta)\,.\end{equation}Overall,asufficientstep-sizeconditiontoguaranteesimultaneousincreaseofpass@$k$anddecreaseofpass@1(i.e.,inparticular$J_1(\theta^+)&lt;J_1(\theta)$and$J_k(\theta^+)&gt;J_k(\theta)$when$\nabla J_k(\theta) \neq 0$)is:\begin{equation}0&lt;\eta\leq\min\left(\frac{\delta(\theta)}{C_{2}},\frac{1}{L_{k}}\right)\,,\end{equation}andthisconcludestheproof.\qed\end@proof\par\par\lx@newpage\par\@@numbered@section{appendix}{toc}{Additional Experiments}
\par\par\@@numbered@section{subsection}{toc}{More details regarding experimental setting}
\par\noindent{Sampling configuration.} For each problem, we generate $k=32$ independent responses using temperature sampling with temperature $T=0.7$ and nucleus sampling with $p=0.95$. Responses are evaluated using exact match against ground truth answers and binary rewards indicate correctness.
\par\par\par\@@numbered@section{subsection}{toc}{Threshold robustness analysis}
\par Figure\penalty\ \ref{fig:all_threshold_configs_llama} shows gradient misalignment across all 6 additional threshold configurations for DeepSeek-R1-Distill-Llama-8B (beyond the main text example of $\delta_{1}=0.85$, $\delta_{2}=0.10$). All combinations exhibit negative inner products (range: $-0.49$ to $-0.65$), demonstrating that the misalignment phenomenon is robust to threshold choices.
\par\begin{figure}\centering\begin{subfigure}[310.4979pt]\centering\includegraphics[width=345.0pt]{Figs/deepseek_llama_threshold_variations_2000_k32/1x3_delta1_0.80_delta2_0.10.pdf}
