Since </mtext></mrow><mo lspace="0.167em" rspace="0.167em">∇</mo><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">J</mi><mi mathvariant="normal">k</mi></msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>θ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>=</mo><msub><mi>𝔼</mi><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">x</mi><mo>∼</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒟</mi></mrow></msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">w</mi><mi mathvariant="normal">k</mi></msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">p</mi><mi>θ</mi></msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo rspace="0.167em" stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo rspace="0.167em">∇</mo><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">p</mi><mi>θ</mi></msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow><mrow><mtext class="ltx_mathvariant_italic"> as shown in (</mtext><mtext class="ltx_mathvariant_italic"><a href="#S2.E2" title="Equation 2 ‣ 2 Preliminaries ‣ Why Pass@k Optimization Can Degrade Pass@1: Prompt Interference in LLM Post-training" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">2</span></a></mtext><mtext class="ltx_mathvariant_italic">), the chain rule gives the Hessian of </mtext></mrow><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">J</mi><mi mathvariant="normal">k</mi></msub><mtext class="ltx_mathvariant_italic"> for every </mtext><mi>θ</mi><mo>∈</mo><msup><mi>ℝ</mi><mi mathvariant="normal">d</mi></msup><mrow><mtext class="ltx_mathvariant_italic">, </mtext><mtext class="ltx_mathvariant_italic">
<table class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

